# ADR-2607162135: kotoba-lang/io-libp2p gains a real TCP gossip/bitswap transport (Phase 4), built on kotoba-lang/wire

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Problem

This is Phase 4 of the net/turn/dtn shared-lib consolidation, the second
of two \"give the protocol-semantics-only library real I/O\" phases
(Phase 3a/3b did this for TURN). The repo referred to throughout this ADR
series' earlier documents as `kotoba-lang/net` — pure `.cljc` gossip
fanout/dedup (`kotoba.net.gossip`) and bitswap want/have + delta-sync
(`kotoba.net.bitswap`) semantics, explicitly \"no real network I/O ... left
to a future native adapter\" per its own README — needed that adapter
built, sharing `kotoba-lang/wire`'s transport plumbing rather than
reinventing it, the same dependency-efficiency demonstrated for
`kotoba-lang/dtn` (Phase 2) and `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` (Phase 3b).

## Decision

### Decision 0 (recorded before the substantive work): `kotoba-lang/net` is a GitHub redirect alias; the canonical name is `kotoba-lang/io-libp2p`

While registering this phase's pin advance in the manifest, `gh api
repos/kotoba-lang/net` resolved to `full_name: \"kotoba-lang/io-libp2p\"` —
the same rename pattern this series already found for
`kotoba-lang/turn` → `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` (ADR-2607161956
Decision 3). `manifest/repos.edn` already had the correct mapping recorded
in its `:path-overrides` (`\"orgs/kotoba-lang/net\" → \"orgs/kotoba-lang/io-libp2p\"`),
and `io-libp2p` was already a registered west project — this ADR's own
manifest change is a plain 1-line pin advance on the EXISTING `io-libp2p`
entry, not a new registration (an initial attempt to add
`orgs/kotoba-lang/net` as a fresh `:extra-projects` entry was made and then
reverted once the override was found, to avoid a redundant/confusing
duplicate path in the policy file). The implementing agent's own work used
the `kotoba-lang/net` alias throughout (clone/push both worked via GitHub's
redirect, same as the `turn` alias did in Phase 1) — harmless for the
actual git operations, but future references in this ADR series should say
`kotoba-lang/io-libp2p`. This ADR's title and body use the canonical name
throughout despite the source material (the agent's own commit messages,
its prior README) still saying \"net\" in places — a naming-hygiene follow-up
(updating in-repo self-references to the canonical name) is additive,
optional future work, not required to land this ADR.

### Decision 1: `kotoba.net.transport.tcp` — real gossip fanout and bitswap request/response, entirely on `kotoba-lang/wire`

`start-node!`/`stop-node!`/`publish!`/`want!`/`request-commits-since!`,
dispatching inbound frames on an explicit `:kind` envelope tag
(`:gossip`/`:bitswap-want`/`:bitswap-commits-since`) decoded via
`kotoba.wire.edn`, delivered via `kotoba.wire.tcp`'s `start-server!`/
`connect-or-reuse!`/`send-framed!` — the exact same API `kotoba-lang/dtn`'s
transport already consumes (Decision confirmed by direct reuse, not
independent reimplementation). `kotoba.net.gossip`/`kotoba.net.bitswap`'s
existing pure semantics and API were not modified — this ADR is a pure
consumer of them, matching the \"wrap a pure domain-computation library,
don't reimplement its algorithm inline\" pattern this workspace already
established for `kotoba-lang/apn` (ADR-2607084700).

For bitswap, the local \"what content do I have\" (`:have-set`) and \"what's
my commit history\" (`:commit-log`) are accepted as `start-node!` options
rather than owned by the transport — correctly treating \"what content
exists locally\" as a host/application concern the transport layer has no
business deciding, the same \"host injects every concrete capability\"
discipline `koe`'s dialog loop and `kotoba.dtn.dtn.gateway`'s structural
decoupling both already use.

### Decision 2: two real friction points found in `gossip.cljc`, worked around at the transport layer without modifying the pure library

1. `content-hash`'s ClojureScript branch requires `goog.crypt.Sha256`,
   which `nbb` doesn't bundle (only `shadow-cljs`-built browser/Node
   targets provide it). A local `src/goog/crypt/Sha256.cljs` shim, backed
   by Node's `node:crypto` core module (verified against real SHA-256 test
   vectors), fills this gap for nbb specifically — the same
   no-npm-dependency discipline every repo in this consolidation series has
   followed.
2. `route-message`/`gossip-fanout` build an exclude set as literal
   `#{from self}` — which throws `Duplicate key` (confirmed genuine
   Clojure/ClojureScript/SCI set-literal behavior, not an nbb quirk)
   whenever `:from` equals `:self`, exactly the shape a locally-originated
   `publish!` naturally produces (a node publishing its own message has
   `:from` = its own id = `:self`). Worked around via a `safe-from` helper
   in the transport layer that substitutes `nil` for `:from` when it would
   equal `:self` (`nil` is never a real peer-id, so the exclude set stays
   correct) — per this task's explicit instruction not to modify
   `gossip.cljc`'s existing pure API, this is recorded as a real, findable
   bug in `kotoba-lang/net`/`io-libp2p` itself, not silently absorbed by a
   workaround that hides it. A follow-up ADR fixing `gossip.cljc` directly
   (e.g. building the exclude set via `(disj #{from} self)`-shaped logic
   instead of a literal set) is legitimate, separable future work.

### Decision 3: verified with real multi-process gossip fanout, including a negative control proving the dedup assertion isn't vacuous

Demo scenario 1 spawns two of three gossip nodes as genuine separate `nbb`
OS processes in a mesh topology (each node aware of the others), publishes
from the third (in-process) node, and confirms each spawned process
receives the message exactly once — despite the mesh topology creating a
genuine redundant re-forward path between the two spawned nodes. The
implementing agent additionally ran a **negative control**: temporarily
disabling the seen-cache dedup gate flipped the assertion to a real
observed FAILURE (count=2), confirming the \"exactly once\" assertion
actually exercises `route-message`'s dedup logic rather than passing
vacuously for an unrelated reason. This workspace does not commonly see
demos include their own negative controls; recorded here as a concrete
positive practice worth naming, not just noting the demo passed.

## Verification

- `clojure -M:test` → 12 tests / 53 assertions (baseline 8/40, +4 tests/+13 assertions for the new pure `kotoba.net.transport.envelope` helpers), 0 failures. `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 1 warning — a **pre-existing**, unrelated `clojure.string` unused-require in `gossip.cljc` (not introduced by this ADR; independently confirmed present both before and after this ADR's changes). Independently re-run from a cold clone — identical results.
- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../wire/src:../bytes/src\" test/kotoba/net/transport/tcp_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 3/3 scenarios passed`, exit 0. Scenario 1: real 3-node mesh gossip fanout with genuine dedup (negative-control-verified, see Decision 3). Scenario 2: real `:bitswap-want`→`:bitswap-have` round-trip over TCP matching the pure `bitswap/respond-to-want` output exactly, including correct exclusion of an unavailable CID. Scenario 3: real `:bitswap-commits-since`→`:bitswap-commits` delta-sync round-trip matching pure `bitswap/commits-since` exactly. Independently re-run from a cold clone by the orchestrating session — output identical to the implementing agent's report.
- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/io-libp2p` (public; still resolvable via the `kotoba-lang/net` GitHub redirect), commit `b87f2605737d6234d8fbe2657cba6375e0fe5f48`.
- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced on the pre-existing `io-libp2p` entry via `--entry io-libp2p` (1-line diff, `863284b926a3→b87f2605737d`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`); `manifest/repos.edn` unchanged (an initial incorrect `:extra-projects` addition was made and reverted per Decision 0, since the canonical entry already existed). Landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching every prior ADR in this series.

## Consequences

- Both halves of the net/turn shared-lib consolidation plan's \"give the
  protocol-semantics libraries real I/O\" goal are now closed:
  `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` relays real UDP traffic (Phase 3b),
  `kotoba-lang/io-libp2p` delivers real gossip/bitswap traffic over real
  TCP (this ADR) — both built on the same `kotoba-lang/wire` foundation
  `kotoba-lang/dtn`'s own transport already uses, demonstrating the
  dependency-efficiency goal at three independent full-service consumers
  now, not merely shared utility functions.
- A real, previously-undiscovered bug in `kotoba.net.gossip` (the
  `#{from self}` duplicate-key crash on self-originated publishes) is now
  known and documented, with a working transport-layer mitigation in
  place — but the pure library itself still carries the defect until a
  follow-up fixes it directly (Decision 2).
- The `kotoba-lang/net` → `kotoba-lang/io-libp2p` naming discovery
  (Decision 0) means any FUTURE work in this ADR series referencing this
  repo should use the canonical name — this ADR is the second time this
  series has hit exactly this pattern (after `turn`/`org-ietf-turn`),
  suggesting it may be worth a repo-wide sweep for other stale `kotoba-lang/*`
  aliases at some point, though that sweep is not performed here.
- Phase 5 (the originally-motivating gap: wiring `kotoba-lang/dtn` to
  actually use `kotoba-lang/io-libp2p` for gossip-based peer/route
  discovery and/or `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` for NAT traversal) remains
  the one phase of this plan not yet started.

## Alternatives considered

- **Fixing the `#{from self}` bug directly in `gossip.cljc` as part of this
  ADR**, rather than working around it in the transport layer. Rejected
  for this pass per this task's explicit scope boundary (don't modify
  `gossip.cljc`'s existing pure API while building its first real
  consumer) — but recorded plainly as a real bug with a clear fix shape
  for a focused follow-up, not silently left undiscoverable behind a
  transport-layer patch.
- **Adding `goog.crypt.Sha256` as an actual npm dependency** instead of a
  local `node:crypto`-backed shim. Rejected: every repo in this
  consolidation series has stayed npm-dependency-free; a small,
  test-vector-verified shim preserves that without pulling in Google
  Closure Library's implementation.
- **Registering `orgs/kotoba-lang/net` as a fresh, separate
  `:extra-projects` entry** instead of recognizing it as the same repo as
  the already-registered `io-libp2p`. Rejected once discovered (see
  Decision 0) — would have created a confusing duplicate path for a single
  underlying repository.

